%T A401981 5,6,5,6,3,7,5,9,4,2,7,7,10,4,4,4,8,4,9,6,8,2,8,7,4,8,4,3,8,5,7,5,6,5,
%U A401981 7,8,2,7,10,8,10,3,4,9,5,4,8,7,5,2,7,8,9
%N A401981 Number of n-bit primes of the form 2^i +- 2^j + 1 with i > j >= 2.
%C A401981 Equivalently, the number of n-bit Solinas primes (A165255) that are congruent to 1 mod 4.
%C A401981 The condition j >= 2 is equivalent to p == 1 (mod 4). The primes 2^i + 1 are included, since 2^i + 1 = 2^(i+1) - 2^i + 1.
%C A401981 Examples of primes counted: 8380417 = 2^23 - 2^13 + 1 (in a(23)), 2013265921 = 2^31 - 2^27 + 1 (in a(31)) and 2^64 - 2^32 + 1 (in a(64)).
%C A401981 a(n) >= 1 for 3 <= n <= 902 except n = 609, where a(609) = 0. The maximum value for n <= 902 is a(307) = 22.
%C A401981 The terms for 3 <= n <= 400 were computed with the PARI/GP function isprime, which proves primality, and agree with an independent computation using a BPSW probable-prime test. The terms for 401 <= n <= 902 were computed with the PARI/GP function ispseudoprime (BPSW probable-prime test); no BPSW counterexample is known.
%C A401981 For a prime p counted here, p-1 = 2^j*(2^(i-j) +- 1) with an odd cofactor, so the prime field F_p contains 2^j-th roots of unity (used in number-theoretic transforms).
%C A401981 If 2*j >= n, then p = k*2^j + 1 with odd k < 2^j, so p is a Proth prime; about half of the primes counted by a(n) have this property for n <= 400.
%C A401981 The primes 2^(n-1) + 2^j + 1 with 2 <= j <= n-2 are counted by A095056(n-1), after subtracting 1 if 2^(n-1) + 3 is prime.
%C A401981 Empirically, a(n) is smaller on average when n == 3 (mod 6): for 3 <= n <= 400 the mean of a(n) is 3.84 over the 67 such n, versus 6.97 over the other 331 values of n.
%H A401981 Sajid Khan Hussain, <a href="/A401981/b401981.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 3..902</a>
%H A401981 J. J. Angel Angel and G. Morales-Luna, <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2006/121">Counting Prime Numbers with Short Binary Signed Representation</a>, Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2006/121, 2006.
%H A401981 J. J. Angel Angel and G. Morales-Luna, <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2010/058">Solinas primes of small weight for fixed sizes</a>, Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2010/058, 2010.
%H A401981 Jerome A. Solinas, <a href="https://cacr.uwaterloo.ca/techreports/1999/corr99-39.pdf">Generalized Mersenne Numbers</a>, CORR 99-39, Univ. of Waterloo, 1999.
%H A401981 S. S. Wagstaff Jr., <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/10586458.2001.10504447">Prime numbers with a fixed number of one bits or zero bits in their binary representation</a>, Exp. Math. 10 (2001), 267-273.
%H A401981 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solinas_prime">Solinas prime</a>.
%e A401981 a(5) = 2 because 17 = 2^5 - 2^4 + 1 and 29 = 2^5 - 2^2 + 1 are the 5-bit primes of this form.
%e A401981 a(6) = 3 because 37 = 2^5 + 2^2 + 1, 41 = 2^5 + 2^3 + 1 and 61 = 2^6 - 2^2 + 1.
%o A401981 (PARI) a(n) = my(c=0); for(j=2, n-2, c += isprime(2^n-2^j+1) + isprime(2^(n-1)+2^j+1)); if(n>=4 && isprime(3*2^(n-2)+1), c--); c + isprime(2^(n-1)+1)
%o A401981 (Python)
%o A401981 # isprime below is a BPSW probable-prime test; the PARI program proves primality.
%o A401981 from sympy import isprime
%o A401981 def a(n):
%o A401981     c = 0
%o A401981     for j in range(2, n-1):
%o A401981         c += isprime(2**n - 2**j + 1) + isprime(2**(n-1) + 2**j + 1)
%o A401981     if n >= 4 and isprime(3*2**(n-2) + 1):
%o A401981         c -= 1
%o A401981     return c + isprime(2**(n-1) + 1)
%Y A401981 Cf. A165255, A081091, A095056, A152449.
%K A401981 nonn,base,new
%O A401981 3,3
